From a6cff3febd4f03fb33b4ade1083d6d8d6f9ead99 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sun, 30 Aug 2026 09:27:30 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] Keep the keyboard off what you are typing into
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

edgeToEdgeEnabled tells Android the window no longer fits the system
insets, and a window that does not fit its insets is not resized for the
keyboard — it just gets covered. Every KeyboardAvoidingView in the app
had `behavior` unset on Android, which means it does nothing and leaves
the work to that resize. So the keyboard sat squarely on top of the Ask
composer, the sign-in fields and every form in a modal, with no way to
see what had been typed.

useKeyboardHeight listens for the keyboard events, which still fire and
still carry the height, and the screens shrink themselves by that much
by hand — doing what adjustResize used to do for us. react-native-
keyboard-controller is the better long-term answer and is also a native
module: a new package, a new build, no Expo Go.

Applied in Screen, so every screen built on it is covered at once, and
then by hand in Ask, sign-in, onboarding and the three modals, which do
not use it. Ask also follows the thread down as the keyboard arrives,
and drops its tab-bar clearance while the tab bar is behind the
keyboard rather than leaving a dead strip above it.

Also: hitSlop on the icon-only controls. A 19px glyph in a box the same
size is the classic "I tapped it and nothing happened".

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017qsfq3JXWhwEdcaLUroXZy
